WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.930
E.T. is one of the most critically beloved
and commercially successful films of all time.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:10.760
With a lifetime worldwide box office of almost
$800 million, E.T. could probably still get

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.719
a sequel greenlit in seconds.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:16.390
So why didn't Universal phone home to see
if they could get the little guy back for

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:17.720
a second adventure?

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.610
Here's why E.T. II: Nocturnal Fears never
happened.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.330
Stuck at the starting line

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:28.190
E.T. screenwriter Melissa Mathison struck
up a creative relationship with director Steven

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:32.710
Spielberg while she was dating Harrison Ford
during filming for Raiders of the Lost Ark.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:37.300
According to producer Kathleen Kennedy, Spielberg
was already a fan of her work on The Black

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.879
Stallion, and after they hit it off, Mathison
got the gig for E.T.

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.870
Over $700 million in box office returns later,
Spielberg wanted to see if she could make

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:48.060
lighting strike twice with a follow-up.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.820
But the outline they sketched together was
never anything more than a tentative stab

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:52.820
at a sequel.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:56.840
A writer and director sitting down to bang
out a nine-page treatment for an idea isn't

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.329
anywhere close to pre-production.

00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:00.960
It got very dark

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.350
If E.T. 2 had followed the treatment written
by Spielberg and Mathison, the film would

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.300
have shown a group of aliens descending to
Earth in response to the distress call from

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.020
"Zrek," which turns out to be E.T.'s real
name.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:13.660
Cool, more little E.T.s, right?

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:14.660
Not really.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.640
These "albino mutations," would have red eyes
and razor sharp teeth, kinda like E.T.'s evil

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.630
neighbors or second-cousins or something.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.240
They spend some time in the woods, mutilating
cattle and capturing animals in "light cages"

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.840
before Elliot, Gertie, and their friends show
up mistakenly thinking E.T. is back.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.250
The kids are interrogated and Elliot is tortured
into unconsciousness while crying for E.T.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.250
to save him.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.009
Sounds like the perfect follow-up to a family-friendly
classic, huh?

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:42.570
The treatment has a happy ending, and it's
obviously just a small part of a longer, more

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.920
detailed story.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.619
But still, turning E.T. even partially into
a horror movie seems like a stretch.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:52.780
Not to mention the Hershey Company would probably
not be too thrilled to see Reese's Pieces

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.720
being associated with sadistic intergalactic
torturers.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:59.760
"Oh, Reeses Pieces"

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.920
Spielberg's first sequel

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:07.060
Steven Spielberg famously hated the idea of
sequels for a long time, having declined Universal's

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.760
request for another Jaws film for years...before
they finally made one anyway without him.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.280
His treatment for E.T. II: Nocturnal Fears
was supposedly drafted just days after the

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.290
original arrived in theaters, and might have
been little more than the byproduct of that

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.910
initial excitement.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.750
Spielberg eventually came around to the idea
of sequels when he filmed Indiana Jones and

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.700
the Temple of Doom three years later.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.650
But it should be pointed out that he's only
ever done four sequels throughout his career,

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.930
and they've been for two of the biggest movie
franchises of all time — Indiana Jones and

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:36.930
Jurassic Park.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:41.200
This is a guy who chooses his projects wisely,
and the story of E.T.'s sadistic relatives

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.410
clearly didn't fit into his artistic vision.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:45.410
One and done

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.630
E.T. is a timeless classic — and a fully
realized story with likeable characters, great

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.709
visual effects, and above all, a feel-good
resolution about friendship and love that

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:56.459
audiences of all kinds can appreciate.

00:02:56.459 --> 00:02:59.950
Spielberg said it himself in an interview
with Empire Magazine in 2009:

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.490
"E.T. is a closed story.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.260
It had a beginning, middle, and a definite
ending, and we had nowhere to take it except

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.260
to go home with him.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.080
Nor did I want to bring him back to Earth
for a second time."

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.690
E.T isn't an action hero who's only just shown
us the beginning of what he can do.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.019
Making an E.T. sequel would have been akin
to making It's A Wonderful Life 2...which

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:18.660
also almost happened.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.580
Thanks, Hollywood.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.739
Spielberg pulled the plug

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:24.920
In a speech to the American Film Institute,
Spielberg said,

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:29.060
"I think a sequel to E.T. would do nothing
but rob the original of its virginity.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.250
People only remember the latest episode, while
the pilot tarnishes."

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.239
...which is quite a reverential way to talk
about a movie featuring a cross-dressing,

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:39.409
candy-binging alien who communicates through
a Speak &amp; Spell.

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:41.890
But it also begs the question: When did he
change his mind?

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.440
And why didn't he give George Lucas this memo
decades ago?

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.480
"I'll try spinning, that's a good trick."

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.769
Thanks for watching!

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:54.540
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.890
And leave us a comment to let us know if you'd
still like to see a sequel to E.T...

